# Change Velocity

How frequently code changes affects stability vs adaptability. Stable systems benefit from optimization; rapidly changing systems benefit from simplicity.

## Options

VelocityLevels {
  Stable => Rarely changes, mostly bug fixes
  SlowEvolution => Planned changes, quarterly features
  ActiveDevelopment => Regular features, weekly/biweekly releases
  RapidIteration => Daily changes, experimenting, pivoting
  Prototype => Trying things, direction unclear
}

## AI Behavior Calibration

BehaviorsByVelocity {
  Stable => minimal changes, preserve patterns, test before modify, warn side effects
  SlowEvolution => clean implementations, appropriate abstraction, refactor with features
  ActiveDevelopment => maintainable solutions, reasonable abstractions, fast tests
  RapidIteration => simple direct solutions, avoid abstraction, wait for patterns, easy-to-delete
  Prototype => maximum simplicity, inline everything, skip refactors, fastest path to results
}

## Velocity × Code Quality

| Velocity | Abstraction | DRY | Testing | Refactoring |
|----------|-------------|-----|---------|-------------|
| Stable | Preserve | Preserve | Add before change | Minimal |
| Slow | Appropriate | Yes | Maintain | With features |
| Active | Moderate | Yes | Fast tests | Incremental |
| Rapid | Minimal | Wait | Critical paths | When patterns emerge |
| Prototype | None | No | Skip | Never |

Constraints {
  Rapid/Prototype: prefer simple, deletable code over "proper" abstractions.
  Stable: understand existing code before changing.
  Active: balance speed and quality.
}
